[section:design Design Notes]

This chapter records the design decisions that are not obvious from the
reference documentation alone.

* [link boost_taar.design.why_header_only Why header-only?]: what would
  be different if Taar were a compiled library.
* [link boost_taar.design.tag_invoke Tag invoke as the extension story]:
  why `tag_invoke` overloads instead of virtual functions or template
  specialisations.
* [link boost_taar.design.soft_vs_hard Soft errors vs hard errors]:
  what gets turned into an HTTP response and what tears the connection
  down.
* [link boost_taar.design.matchers Matchers as expression templates]:
  why operator overloading and how it interacts with route dispatch
  cost.
* [link boost_taar.design.async The asynchronous model]: how
  coroutines thread through the session, the handlers, and streaming
  responses.
* [link boost_taar.design.layers Layers, not a framework]: how REST,
  JSON-RPC, MCP and OpenAPI sit on one HTTP transport.
* [link boost_taar.design.contexts Contexts and keyed state]: the three
  scopes of state a handler can ask for, and how `context_store` keeps
  them safe.
* [link boost_taar.design.interceptors Interceptors and guards]: where
  cross-cutting checks run, how they short-circuit, and how they hand
  attributes to handlers.
* [link boost_taar.design.errors Error mapping per surface]: the one
  error value (`failure`), and how each layer maps it to its protocol.
* [link boost_taar.design.protocols Protocol decisions]: the choices made
  where JSON-RPC, MCP and OpenAPI leave room.

[section:why_header_only Why header-only?]

Every public type in Taar is a template that instantiates differently for
each user-supplied handler signature, request body, response body, and
executor. There is no implementation that would benefit from being
precompiled into a library: separating declaration from definition would
force users to instantiate every combination explicitly. A header-only
distribution keeps the build simple and matches the rest of the
matcher/handler ecosystem on top of Boost.Beast and Boost.Asio.

[endsect]

[section:tag_invoke Tag invoke as the extension story]

The two main customisation points, `response_from` and `rest_arg_cast`,
are exposed as `tag_invoke` overloads rather than virtual functions or
class template specialisations.

`tag_invoke` is found by ordinary argument-dependent lookup, which means a
user-defined return type or argument type can opt in to Taar by declaring a
free function next to the type itself, without touching any namespace owned
by the library. This is the same pattern used by `boost::json::value_from`
and the C++26 sender/receiver proposal.

[endsect]

[section:soft_vs_hard Soft errors vs hard errors]

A /soft error/ is something that an HTTP handler should be able to turn
into a normal HTTP response: a missing query parameter, an unparseable
path argument, a body that fails to deserialise. A /hard error/ is
something that takes the connection down: a peer disconnect mid-read, a
write that fails because the socket is closed.

Both have pluggable handlers installed on a session via
`set_soft_error_handler` and `set_hard_error_handler`, and both receive a
`taar::failure const&` (see
[link boost_taar.design.errors Error mapping per surface]). The session
wraps whatever escapes a request handler into a failure exactly once and
hands it to the soft handler. The default soft handler answers library
argument and body errors with `400` and the failure's text, a body limit
with `413`, and escalates everything else (user exceptions, internal
codes) to the hard error handler, which sees the same failure; the
connection is then closed. Transport errors go to the hard handler
directly. Applications that throw their own exceptions from handlers
install a soft error handler that recognises them with `f.as<E>()` (see
`example/rest_crud_server.cpp`).

[endsect]

[section:matchers Matchers as expression templates]

A matcher composition like

    method == http::verb::get && target == "/api/{id}" && version == 11

is not evaluated eagerly. Each operator returns a small object that holds
its operands by value and carries the combined matching logic. The result
is one expression-template object per registered route, with no heap
allocation and full inlining at the call site.

This is the same technique that __Boost_Spirit__, __Boost_Hana__, and
__Boost_Proto__ rely on. The cost is that diagnostics for an ill-formed
matcher are longer than they would be with a runtime API; the benefit is
that route dispatch is a flat sequence of `if`s with no virtual calls.

The same expression also builds a type-level description of itself: the
`metadata_type` of the resulting operand is an AST of leaf tags joined by
`and_`, `or_` and `not_`, and the operand stores what each leaf captured
(the verb, the path template, the header value). That is the only
information the OpenAPI generator uses, so matching logic and
documentation can never disagree.

[endsect]

[section:async The asynchronous model]

Taar is asynchronous from top to bottom, built on __Asio__ C++20
coroutines. There is no thread-per-connection pool and no callback
plumbing in user code; concurrency is expressed with `co_await`.

[heading Everything is a coroutine]

The TCP acceptor (`taar::server::tcp`) and the per-connection session
(`taar::session::http`) are both `taar::awaitable<void>` coroutines
spawned with `boost::asio::co_spawn`. One in-flight connection is one
suspended coroutine frame, not a thread, so a single-threaded
`io_context` already serves many simultaneous connections; running the
`io_context` from several threads scales it across cores.

`taar::awaitable<T>` is an alias for
`boost::asio::awaitable<T, any_io_executor>`, which is exactly
`boost::asio::awaitable<T>`: the two are the same type and interchangeable,
so a handler may return either and may `co_await` any Asio awaitable. The
same coroutine runs on a plain `io_context` executor or on a strand.
`taar::use_awaitable` and `taar::use_awaitable_tuple` are the matching
completion tokens (throwing, and returning `(error_code, result...)`).

[heading Executors and strands]

The servers run every connection on its own strand, made from the
executor connections are created from, so a multi-threaded `io.run()`
needs no further care inside one connection: its operations never run
concurrently with each other. Inside a handler,
`co_await boost::asio::this_coro::executor` is that connection's strand.
Work spawned on it (`co_spawn(co_await this_coro::executor, ...)`) is
serialised with the connection; work spawned on the `io_context`
executor runs in parallel with it. Objects shared between connections (a
cache, a counter, a store) are touched from several strands, and
therefore from several threads, so they need their own synchronisation;
`context_store` and `async_channel` are thread-safe for that reason.

The plain `server::tcp(host, port, session, signals)` makes connection
strands from the executor it is spawned on, so spawn it on the
`io_context` (or thread pool) executor itself. Spawned on a strand, every
connection strand would be layered on that strand and all connections
would be serialised through it. When the server has to run on a strand,
or connections should run on another executor, use the overload that takes
the connection executor explicitly:

    boost::asio::co_spawn(
        boost::asio::make_strand(io),
        taar::server::tcp(io.get_executor(), "0.0.0.0", "8080", session, signals),
        boost::asio::detached);

`server::tls` has the same pair of overloads. The acceptor itself always
runs on a strand of its own.

Registration is configuration, not run-time state: the functions that add
routes and hooks to a session or a JSON-RPC or MCP endpoint
(`register_request_handler`, `register_raw_handler`, the error handler and
filter setters, `method`, `tool`, `resource`, `prompt` and the like) are
not synchronised against serving. Configure everything before `io.run()`
starts the server.

Handlers see plain __Beast__ and __Asio__ types. A session handler passed
to `server::tcp` is called with a `boost::asio::ip::tcp::socket` already
moved onto the connection strand, and a raw handler receives a
`boost::beast::tcp_stream&&` (or `session::tls_stream&&`, a plain
`beast::ssl_stream<beast::tcp_stream>`) and a `beast::flat_buffer&&`.
These streams keep Asio's default completion token, so pass
`taar::use_awaitable` or `taar::use_awaitable_tuple` to their operations.
`taar::rebind_executor<T>`, which rebinds an I/O object to the tuple token
by default, remains as a convenience for the library's private objects and
for client sockets.

[heading Synchronous or asynchronous handlers]

A request handler wrapped by `taar::handler::rest` may return its value
directly /or/ return a `taar::awaitable<T>`. The session detects which
and does the right thing: a plain value is converted to a response
immediately; an awaitable is `co_await`ed first. This means a handler
that needs to talk to a database or call another service simply becomes
a coroutine (its signature changes from `T` to `taar::awaitable<T>` and
its body gains `co_await`/`co_return`) with no change to how it is
registered or how its arguments are extracted.

The soft-error handler (see
[link boost_taar.design.soft_vs_hard Soft errors vs hard errors]) may
likewise be a coroutine, so error responses can also be produced from
asynchronous work.

[heading Streaming responses]

For responses that are produced incrementally (server-sent events, a
large file, the output of a long-running job), a handler can return a
`taar::async_generator<T>` or a `taar::chunked_response<T>`. These are
themselves coroutines: the body `co_yield`s each piece of the response
and may `co_await` between pieces, while `taar::chunked_response`
additionally lets the producer set the HTTP status and headers (by
`co_yield`ing `taar::set_status` / `taar::set_header`) before the first
chunk goes on the wire. The session drives the generator and writes each
yielded value using HTTP chunked transfer-encoding.

Request bodies stream too. A handler whose request type is
`streaming_request` is invoked before the body is read and pulls it
through a `body_reader` straight from the connection, so the session's
body limit is bypassed and memory stays bounded by the handler's buffer. A
body left unread when the response is produced closes the connection
rather than being drained, since draining an arbitrarily large upload to
keep one connection alive is rarely the right trade.

`multipart/form-data` is parsed by one incremental scanner used both for
buffered forms (`parse_multipart`) and for streamed ones
(`multipart_reader`). The scanner holds back only as many bytes as a
delimiter could span, so a file part is delivered in pieces regardless of
how the bytes arrived; a malformed body is an `invalid_request_format`
soft error, and a part without a `name` is treated as malformed.
`multipart_reader::next_part` discards whatever is left of the current
part, so a handler may skip fields it does not care about.

Server-push streams that wait for events produced elsewhere (the MCP
event stream, a chat room) read from a `taar::async_channel<T>`: any
thread pushes, the generator `co_await`s `async_receive`, and `close()`
ends the stream.

[heading Cancellation and graceful shutdown]

Shutdown is cooperative. A `taar::cancellation_signals` pool hands a
cancellation slot to every spawned coroutine; emitting on the pool
(typically from a `SIGINT`/`SIGTERM` handler) stops the acceptor and
cancels the idle read on every keep-alive connection, so idle connections
close at once. Asio requires a cancellation to be emitted within the
strand of the operation it cancels, so the servers register each
connection's slot together with its strand and `emit()` dispatches the
emission there; it may therefore be called from any thread, including
one that is not running the `io_context`. The plain `slot()` form, which
emits inline, remains for single-threaded use. A slot bound to the server
coroutine by its spawner still stops it: the cancellation is re-emitted
through the acceptor's strand rather than applied inline.

A request whose handler is running when the signal arrives is allowed to
finish: the session shields a plain (non-streaming) handler from
cancellation, records that shutdown was requested, writes the handler's
response with `Connection: close`, and ends the connection. Streaming
handlers stay cancellable, because an open-ended stream would otherwise
hold the process up; the cancellation propagates into the generator's
pending `co_await` and the connection is dropped.

Cancellation reaches nested work on every path, and the frames it
interrupts unwind (RAII runs); `test/test_cancellation.cpp` checks each
one and `example/cancellation_server.cpp` shows them side by side:

* /shutdown/ cancels a streaming handler wherever it is suspended: a
  timer between chunks, a helper coroutine several `co_await`s deep, a
  spliced inner stream, an `async_generator`, both branches of `a && b`
  or `a || b`, or a loop polling `this_coro::cancellation_state`. Plain
  handlers, including one reading a streamed request body, are shielded:
  they finish, and their answer carries `Connection: close`. A shielded
  body read waits for the client, at most the body timeout (see
  [link boost_taar.design.async.timeouts Timeouts]), so a client that stops
  sending mid-upload holds that connection for that long and is then
  answered `408`.
* /client disconnect/ during a stream is noticed at the stream's next
  write; the generator is then destroyed with its nested frames. A stream
  suspended in a long wait between chunks keeps waiting until that wait
  ends, since the session is not reading the socket while it streams.
* /JSON-RPC/ `endpoint::cancel(id)` emits on the request's
  `request_scope`, interrupting every `co_await` in the method; a method
  can also poll `scope.cancelled()`. The answer is a `-32800` error.
* /MCP/ `notifications/cancelled` does the same for a tool call, over
  HTTP (the cancelled call is answered `202` with no body) and stdio (no
  reply line), as well as for a tool suspended in `elicit()`.

`basic_http` is parameterised on its stream type. The default is Beast's
`tcp_stream`; `session::https` instantiates it over
`beast::ssl_stream<tcp_stream>`, and `server::tls` owns the handshake so
the session never sees a half-established connection. On exit a TLS
session sends `close_notify` (bounded by a five second timer) before the
TCP half-close. Nothing in the handler layer depends on the stream type,
so every route, context and protocol layer works unchanged over TLS.

[heading:timeouts Timeouts]

A connection is bounded by four timeouts, modelled on nginx and set on the
session (`set_idle_timeout`, `set_header_timeout`, `set_body_timeout`,
`set_send_timeout`; zero disables one):

[table
[[Timeout][Runs][On expiry][Default]]
[[idle (`keepalive_timeout`)]
 [while waiting for the first byte of a request, the first one or the
  next on a keep-alive connection]
 [the connection is closed silently][60 s]]
[[header (`client_header_timeout`)]
 [from the first byte of a request until its header is complete]
 [`408 Request Timeout` with `Connection: close`, through the response
  filter, then close][30 s]]
[[body (`client_body_timeout`)]
 [for each read of a request body, buffered by the session or made
  through `body_reader` / `multipart_reader`: the longest the client may
  stay silent]
 [`408` (a streaming handler sees a failure carrying
  `beast::error::timeout`, which the default soft error handler answers
  with `408`), then close][60 s]]
[[send (`send_timeout`)]
 [for each write: the longest a write may stay blocked, for example on a
  client that stopped reading]
 [the connection is closed][60 s]]
]

Only time spent in the guarded operation counts. A handler's execution
never does, and neither does the time a streaming generator spends
between chunks, so a server-sent-events stream with long gaps needs no
special configuration. The idle and send timeouts use the stream's own
expiry (`expires_after` on the lowest layer, also under TLS), set before
the operation and cleared (`expires_never`) after it; Beast closes the
socket when it fires. The header and body timeouts must leave the
connection open for the `408`, so they use a timer that cancels the
pending read on the socket instead of closing it. That cancellation does
not travel through the coroutine's cancellation slot, so it also bounds
the body reads that are shielded from shutdown: a client that stalls
mid-upload delays a graceful shutdown by at most the body timeout. The
TLS handshake keeps its own timeout (`server::tls`), and the close
sequence (`close_notify`, lingering close) keeps its timers.

An expired timeout is the client's doing, not a server failure: it is
not reported to the hard error handler.

[heading The `Date` header]

Every response the session writes carries `Date` (RFC 9110 6.6.1) unless
the handler or the response filter already set one, including error
answers, `304`, HEAD responses, the header of chunked responses and the
`400`/`408` protocol answers. The value is formatted at most once per
second per thread, so it costs neither a lock nor a formatting per
response. `set_date_header(false)` turns it off. A raw handler returning
a `message_generator` writes what it produces.

[heading Unmatched requests and HEAD]

A request that matches no route is answered `405 Method Not Allowed`
with an `Allow` header when some route accepts the same path under another
method, and `404` otherwise (including when the method is accepted but a
header, cookie or version constraint rejected the request). `HEAD` is served through a matching `GET`
route: the handler runs with the HEAD request and only the response
header is written, so `Content-Length` is what the GET would carry. Both
behaviours use the matcher metadata (a route must be a conjunction of one
`target ==` and `method ==` constraints to take part) and both can be
switched off with `set_method_not_allowed` and `set_head_from_get`.

Responses are always aligned with the request they answer: same HTTP
version, and keep-alive only if the request allows it, the handler did not
disable it, and the server is not shutting down. Pipelined requests are
served in order from one per-connection buffer. A peer that closes an idle
keep-alive connection is not an error; a generator that throws after
chunks are on the wire truncates the response and reports the exception
to the hard error handler.

[endsect]

[section:layers Layers, not a framework]

The HTTP session, the matchers and `handler::rest` form the transport and
REST layer. JSON-RPC, MCP and OpenAPI are separate objects layered on top
of it rather than modes of the session:

* each layer is user-owned and keeps its own registry, so a program can
  have several JSON-RPC endpoints or none;
* each layer hands the session an ordinary handler for one route, so
  routing, keep-alive, limits, contexts and error handling are shared;
* MCP composes a JSON-RPC endpoint instead of re-implementing it, and
  custom MCP methods are plain JSON-RPC methods;
* REST has no registry object of its own: routes are registered directly
  on the session, and the OpenAPI generator observes those registrations.

The binding machinery is shared too. `jsonrpc::detail::bind_method`
turns a callable plus argument providers into an invoker and a parameter
description; `handler::rest`, JSON-RPC methods, MCP tools and MCP
resources all use the same context-injection rules and the same
`rest_arg_cast` conversions, which is what lets one `int add(calc&, int,
int)` serve all three surfaces unchanged. Providers that read the
HTTP request (`header_arg`, `cookie_arg`, `query_arg`) are accepted on
every surface as well, because every call type carries the request it
arrived on.

[endsect]

[section:contexts Contexts and keyed state]

A handler can ask for state in three scopes by declaring leading
parameters:

* /connection/: `basic_http<T>` constructs one `T` when a connection is
  accepted and destroys it when the connection closes. Keep-alive makes
  this the natural home for per-client counters and caches.
* /keyed/: a `context_store<Key, T>` holds one `T` per key with idle
  expiry; `with_session` resolves the key from a cookie or header
  (minting one when allowed), locks the entry for the duration of the
  request and injects the `T`. Under a streaming handler the lock covers
  only the call that produces the stream; the chunks then flow unlocked,
  so one client's open stream never blocks its other requests, and the
  minted key travels in the stream's headers. The MCP endpoint uses the
  same store for its sessions keyed by `Mcp-Session-Id`.
* /none/: handlers without context parameters work in every scope.

Contexts are matched by type, in any order, so adding a context parameter
never shifts the argument providers. `T&` and `T const&` are preferred;
`std::shared_ptr<T>` is accepted for handlers that need to retain the
object beyond the request.

`context_store` is designed around three facts: requests for the same key
may arrive concurrently on different connections and threads, so each
entry has an asynchronous lock that serialises them while requests for
different keys run in parallel; entries must disappear when clients go
away without telling us, so every access touches a timestamp and sweeps
expired entries opportunistically (no background timer); and a locked or
in-flight entry must never be swept. Keys minted by the store are 128
random bits as hexadecimal, safe in cookies and headers. Set-Cookie uses
`Path=/; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax` by default and never sets an expiry: the
store's idle timeout is the session lifetime.

[endsect]

[section:interceptors Interceptors and guards]

Authentication, request ids, rate limits and similar cross-cutting checks
need to run before the handler, see the request, and either let it pass,
attach something for the handler, or answer it themselves. Taar has two
places for them.

[heading Session interceptors]

`basic_http::add_interceptor(fn)` runs `fn` on every request;
`add_interceptor(matcher, fn)` on the requests the matcher accepts. They
run in registration order right after the request header is parsed:

* before a route is selected, so they apply to every route, raw
  (WebSocket) handlers included;
* before the `405 Method Not Allowed`, `404` and HEAD-from-GET handling,
  so an unauthenticated client learns nothing about which paths or
  methods exist;
* before any body byte is read.

`fn` takes the session's contexts by type (as handlers do), then
`(http::request_header<> const&, matcher::context&)`, and returns
`taar::interceptor_result`, an optional response, directly or as an
awaitable. `taar::proceed()` is `std::nullopt`: the next interceptor runs,
then routing. `taar::reject(status, text)` builds a `text/plain`
response; any response short-circuits: later interceptors and the
handler do not run.

A rejection is written like any other response: its version and
keep-alive follow the request and the response filter runs on it, so a
browser sees the CORS headers on a `401` and can read it. The request
body has not been read; when the request declares one the connection
closes after the rejection rather than reading and discarding a body the
server has already refused (an upload of a gigabyte gets its `401` at
once). An interceptor that throws is handled like a handler that throws:
the soft error handler receives the failure and can recognise a domain
exception with `f.as<E>()`.

The matcher form evaluates the matcher on the header exactly as a route
does. Path arguments a target template extracts go into a scratch context
that is discarded; the interceptor receives the request's own context.
Two scopes come up constantly:

    // CORS preflights carry no credentials: let them through.
    session.add_interceptor(!(method == http::verb::options), require_token);
    // A public health check.
    session.add_interceptor(!(target == "/health"), require_token);

Scope carefully when several protocols share a session: `DELETE /mcp`
ends an MCP session, so an interceptor meant for `DELETE /tasks/{id}`
should name the target rather than only the method.

[heading Request attributes]

`matcher::context` carries a small type-indexed bag,
`request_attributes`, with `set(value)`, `find<T>()` and `contains<T>()`.
An interceptor stores what it learned (`ctx.attributes.set(principal {...})`)
and the handler that later runs reads it, directly through the context or
with the `attribute_arg<T>()` provider, which works in `rest`,
`jsonrpc::endpoint::method` and `mcp::endpoint::tool` alike. One value is
kept per type, so define a type per attribute rather than storing bare
strings. Routing resets only the path arguments between candidate routes;
attributes survive it. An absent attribute is `argument_not_found`, so a
`std::optional<T>` parameter or `with_default` makes it optional.
`attribute_arg` is not a client input: OpenAPI, the JSON-RPC parameter
list and the MCP `inputSchema` leave it out.

[heading Route guards]

`handler::guard(check, inner)` decorates one route. `check` has the
interceptor signature and runs when the route's handler is invoked; on a
rejection that response is the route's answer (converted to the inner's
response type, or sent as a one-shot chunked response when the inner
streams), otherwise the inner handler runs and sees the attributes the
check set. The guard forwards `params()` and the path-argument names, so
OpenAPI and the registration checks see the inner handler, and it composes
with `with_session` in either order: inside `with_session` the check can
ask for the keyed context.

Choose between the two by when the decision must happen. A guard runs
after routing and after the body has been read (the inner handler
decides how the body is read), so it suits checks that belong to one
route and may depend on its keyed state. A scoped interceptor
(`add_interceptor(method == http::verb::delete_ && target == "/tasks/{id}", check)`)
runs before the body is read and before routing answers, which is what a
check guarding an upload, or hiding which routes exist, needs.

[endsect]

[section:errors Error mapping per surface]

Domain code throws plain exceptions and knows nothing about HTTP, JSON-RPC
or MCP. The library itself reports every error through one value type,
and each layer maps that value to its protocol where it crosses into it,
with a default and a hook.

[heading One error value: `failure`]

`boost::taar::failure` (in `<boost/taar/core/error.hpp>`) carries

* an `error_code` from any category: Taar's own `taar::error`, Asio,
  Beast, Boost.JSON, generic `errc`, `jsonrpc::error`, `mcp::error`, or
  a user category;
* a message for this occurrence that reads on its own ("query parameter
  'tag' occurs more than once", "request body exceeds the read limit of
  100 bytes"). It does not repeat the code's generic text;
* optional structured data, a `boost::json::value`;
* an optional nested cause, so a failure explains itself in layers;
* the source location where it was raised;
* optionally the foreign exception it was made from.

`text()` renders the failure for people: each level prints its message,
or the code's own text when the message is empty, and causes follow in
`(caused by: ...)`. `chain()` adds `[category:value]` tags to every level
and is meant for logs. `is(code)` tests the outermost code, `has(code)`
the whole chain, and `root()` returns the innermost cause.

`failure::from_exception(eptr)` turns any exception into a failure: a
`taar::exception` yields its own failure unchanged; a
`boost::system::system_error` or `std::system_error` yields its code with
the exception's text as the message; any other exception becomes
`taar::error::unknown_exception` with `what()` as the message. In every
case but the first the original exception is kept, so `rethrow()` throws
the user's own type again and `as<E>()` finds it.

[heading `result<T>` and `taar::exception`]

`taar::result<T>` is `boost::system::result<T, failure>`: the
non-throwing shape used by argument providers and parsers. Accessing
`value()` on a failed result throws through Boost.System's ADL hook
`throw_exception_from_error`, which Taar defines for `failure` to throw
`taar::exception`.

`taar::exception` is the one exception type the library throws. It
carries a `failure` (`error()`), and its `what()` is the failure's
`chain()`. Registration-time programmer errors (a duplicate tool name, a
`path_arg` that names no placeholder, an invalid template) are reported
when the handler is registered: duplicate and unknown names as
`std::logic_error`, template and `path_arg` mismatches as
`taar::exception`.

[heading How the binder nests failures]

Argument providers return `taar::result<V>`. An absent argument is
`failure{error::argument_not_found}` with no message; `with_default` and
`std::optional` parameters absorb exactly that code. Any other provider
failure, or an exception thrown by a `rest_arg_cast` (built-in or user),
becomes the cause of an argument-level failure: code
`error::invalid_argument_format`, message
`argument page (position 1) is invalid`, data
`{"argument": "page", "position": 1}`. Its `text()` then reads, for
instance:

[pre argument page (position 1) is invalid (caused by: value 'abc' is not a number (Invalid argument))]

A `constrained`
violation is not wrapped: it is `error::constraint_violated` with a
message such as `page must be >= 1` and data `{"argument": "page"}`.
JSON-RPC and MCP arguments (`rpc_arg`, `mcp_arg`, `uri_arg`) follow the
same scheme.

[heading Mapping by code, per surface]

Each default mapper looks only at the code; none of them inspects
exception types.

[table
[[Surface][Default][Hook]]
[[REST]
 [`http_status_for(f)` decides: Taar's request codes (argument not found
  or ambiguous, invalid argument, request, URL, content type, cookie,
  boolean or number format, constraint violated) -> `400`; Beast
  `body_limit` -> `413`; `header_limit` -> `431`; `beast::error::timeout`
  (a request body read that timed out) -> `408`. A failure mapped to a
  `4xx` status is answered with that status and `f.text()` as a
  `text/plain` body. Everything else maps to `500` and is escalated to
  the hard error handler instead of being answered.]
 [`set_soft_error_handler(h)`, `h(taar::failure const&)` returning
  anything `response_from` accepts, or an awaitable of it]]
[[JSON-RPC]
 [`default_error_mapper(f)`: a `jsonrpc::error` code -> that code with
  the failure's message (or the code's text); a Taar code other than
  `unknown_exception` -> `-32602 Invalid params` with `data` equal to the
  failure's object data plus `"message": text()`, or the `text()` string
  when there is no object data; anything else (user exceptions) ->
  `-32603 Internal error` with the failure's message and no `data`.
  Unknown methods are `-32601`; a cancelled request is `-32800`.]
 [`set_error_mapper(fn)`, `fn(taar::failure const&) -> error_info`]]
[[MCP tools]
 [`default_tool_error_handler(f)`: Taar (except `unknown_exception`),
  `jsonrpc::error` and `mcp::error` codes stay protocol errors and go
  through the JSON-RPC mapper; anything else becomes a result with
  `isError: true` and `f.text()` as its text content.]
 [`set_tool_error_handler(fn)`, with
  `fn(taar::failure const&) -> std::optional<std::string>`; `nullopt`
  means protocol error]]
]

The MCP layer has its own category, `mcp::error`. Where MCP assigns a
wire code the enumerator's value is that code and is sent as is:
`resource_not_found` (`-32002`) and `request_cancelled` (`-32800`). The
other enumerators describe failures of server-to-client requests
(`request`, `elicit`, `create_message`, `list_roots`): `client_error`
when the client answers with a JSON-RPC error (the failure's message is
the client's message, its data `{"code": n, "data": ...}`) and
`unknown_session`. A timeout is `errc::timed_out` and a session that ends
first `errc::operation_canceled`, each with a message naming the method
and the session.

[heading Escalation]

On the REST surface a failure whose status would be `500` (a user
exception, an internal or transport code) is not turned into a response
by the default soft handler: it is escalated to the hard error handler
with the same failure, and the connection is closed. The hard handler is
where an application logs; `f.chain()` gives the tagged rendering with
the whole cause chain, and `f.where()` the raise site. A soft handler or
mapper that throws is treated the same way: the session escalates, and
the JSON-RPC and MCP endpoints fall back to their default mapper, so an
error in error handling cannot take a connection down unexpectedly.

[heading Recognising your own exceptions]

A hook recognises a domain exception without a try/catch ladder with
`failure::as<E>()`, which returns a copy of the kept exception as a
`std::optional<E>` if it is an `E` (or derives from it) and
`std::nullopt` otherwise. It returns a copy, not a pointer, because
whether `std::rethrow_exception` copies the exception object is
unspecified (MSVC copies it), so the address of a caught object cannot
be handed out portably:

    session.set_soft_error_handler([](taar::failure const& f)
    {
        if (auto const e = f.as<not_found>())
            return taar::response_builder(e->what()).set_status(http::status::not_found);
        return taar::response_builder(f.text()).set_status(taar::http_status_for(f));
    });

    rpc.set_error_mapper([](taar::failure const& f) -> taar::jsonrpc::error_info
    {
        if (auto const e = f.as<not_found>()) return {-32004, e->what(), std::nullopt};
        return taar::jsonrpc::default_error_mapper(f);
    });

Domain code that wants a specific mapping without a hook can also throw
`taar::exception{taar::failure{code, message}}` with a code the default
mappers already know.

[endsect]

[section:protocols Protocol decisions]

Where the specifications leave room, Taar makes these choices:

[heading JSON-RPC 2.0]

* Every JSON response, including error objects, is HTTP `200`. Only
  notifications (and all-notification batches) answer `202` with an empty
  body.
* Batches are processed element by element; one failure never aborts its
  siblings. An empty batch is a single `-32600` object. An invalid element
  is answered with `-32600` and its `id` if that was a string, number or
  null.
* Parameters bind by name from a `params` object or by position from a
  `params` array; positions count only the `rpc_arg` providers, so HTTP
  providers mixed into a method do not shift them. A short array leaves
  trailing parameters missing; extra elements and unknown members are
  ignored; a scalar `params` is `-32602`.
* Every request runs under a `request_scope` registered by id (namespaced
  by the MCP session when there is one). `endpoint::cancel(id)` fires its
  cancellation slot, so awaits inside the callable are interrupted; a
  cancelled JSON-RPC request answers `-32800`. `std::optional<T>` and `with_default` make a parameter
  optional. Method names are case-sensitive; registering a name twice
  throws at startup.

[heading MCP]

* Supported protocol revisions are `2025-11-25`, `2025-06-18` and
  `2025-03-26`. A request for a supported version is echoed; anything else
  is answered with the newest supported version, as the specification asks.
  A request carrying an unsupported `MCP-Protocol-Version` header is `400`.
* Stateful endpoints mint `Mcp-Session-Id` on `initialize`; later requests
  without it are `400`, with an unknown or expired id `404`, and `DELETE`
  ends the session. Stateless endpoints issue and require no id.
* JSON-RPC batches are rejected (`-32600`), following the current
  transport. A JSON-RPC response or notification posted by the client is
  acknowledged with `202`.
* Server-to-client requests (`request`, and `create_message`, `elicit`,
  `list_roots` on top of it) go out over the session's event stream (or
  stdout on stdio) with server-side ids; the client's response is matched
  by id and routed to the awaiting coroutine without taking the session
  lock, so a tool can await its own client while it holds the session.
  Other requests from the same session wait meanwhile, so the wait is
  bounded by `server_info::request_timeout` (five minutes by default)
  unless a call passes its own. Failures throw `taar::exception`: an
  error reply carries `mcp::error::client_error` with the client's code
  and data, a timeout `errc::timed_out`, the session ending
  `errc::operation_canceled`. `session_info` carries the client's identity and
  declared `capabilities`, so a tool can check before asking.
  `on_client_notification` hooks notifications the client sends, such as
  `notifications/roots/list_changed`.
* Prompts are registered like tools (`prompt(name, description, callable,
  mcp_arg...)`); a string result becomes one user text message, an array
  is taken as the messages, an object with `messages` is passed through.
  The `prompts` capability is advertised only when prompts exist.
* `resources/subscribe` is stateful-only (the subscription lives in the
  session); `resource_updated(uri)` notifies subscribed sessions, and the
  `*_changed()` helpers broadcast `list_changed`. Stateless endpoints
  answer subscribe with `-32601` and do not advertise it.
* `*/list` methods paginate when `server_info::page_size` is set: cursors
  are opaque strings (`-32602` when invalid), `nextCursor` is present
  while more items remain.
* `tools/call` renders scalar results as text and wraps them as
  `structuredContent: {"result": ...}`; object results are passed through
  as `structuredContent`; `void` yields an empty `content` array. Unknown
  tools and bad arguments are `-32602`; an unknown resource is `-32002`.
* Resource templates follow RFC 6570: `{var}` stops at `/`, `?`, `#`
  and `,`; `{+var}` spans them; `{#var}`, `{.var}`, `{/var}`, `{;var}`,
  `{?a,b}` and `{&a,b}` are optional when their prefix is absent, so one
  template serves `calc://x/{i}` and `calc://x/{i}?format=json`. `uri_arg`
  binds the captured values; an absent optional one is `argument_not_found`,
  which a `std::optional` or `with_default` parameter absorbs.
* `tools/list` carries an `outputSchema` derived from the callable's
  return type with the same wrapping (described structs and string maps
  as is, scalars, strings, enums and sequences under `result`), unless
  `tool_spec::output_schema` sets or disables it. `tool_spec` also adds
  `title` and `annotations` (read-only, destructive, idempotent,
  open-world hints).
* `logging/setLevel` is stored per session (initially
  `server_info::default_log_level`); `log()` and `log_all()` emit
  `notifications/message` only at or above it. The `logging` capability
  is always advertised.
* `completion/complete` consults completers registered per prompt or
  resource-template argument (`complete_prompt`, `complete_resource`);
  an argument without one answers an empty list, an unknown ref is
  `-32602`, and at most 100 values are returned with `hasMore` and
  `total`. The `completions` capability appears once a completer exists.
* The `GET` event stream is one per session (`409` for a second), fed by
  `notify`/`broadcast`; messages queued before a stream opens are kept,
  up to `server_info::stream_history` of them (oldest dropped first), so
  a session that never opens its stream cannot grow without bound. An
  idle stream carries a `: keepalive` comment every
  `server_info::stream_keepalive` (15 s by default), so a dropped
  connection is noticed and the session freed for a reconnect.
  Deleting or sweeping the session ends the stream. `notify` does not
  refresh the session's idle timer. A tool reaches its own stream through
  the injected `session_info`.
* Every stream event carries an `id`; a session keeps the last
  `server_info::stream_history` events (100 by default). A `GET` with
  `Last-Event-ID` first replays the events newer than that id which an
  earlier stream had already taken (those the client may have missed in
  flight), then continues with whatever is still queued, so a
  reconnecting client sees each event once and in order. An event that
  fell out of the bounded history before the reconnect is gone.
* Tools return plain values (text plus `structuredContent`) or the typed
  results in `mcp/content.hpp`: `image`, `audio`, `embedded_resource`,
  `text`, or a full `tool_result` with several items and an explicit
  `isError`. A resource callable may return `blob` for binary contents;
  base64 is produced by `base64_encode` or the `from_bytes` helpers.
* `notifications/cancelled` is handled before the session lock is taken,
  so it can reach a tool that is running; the tool's awaits are
  interrupted and, as the specification asks, no response is sent for the
  cancelled request. `_meta.progressToken` on a request enables
  `request_scope::report_progress`, which emits `notifications/progress`
  on the session's stream; without a token the reports are dropped.
* The stdio transport (`serve_stdio`) runs one implicit session: created
  on `initialize`, removed at end of input. Requests before `initialize`
  are answered `-32600`. Requests run concurrently with reading (still
  serialised per session) so a cancellation can arrive mid-call. Replies
  and notifications share one writer so lines never interleave. When the
  input closes, calls still running finish and their replies are flushed
  before the session is removed and the coroutine returns.

[heading OpenAPI]

* A route is expressible when its matcher is a conjunction of exactly one
  `target == ...` and at least one `method == ...` (several may be joined
  with `||`), plus optional header, cookie and version constraints.
  Negation, `!=` and `||` over paths are rejected at compile time rather
  than documented approximately.
* Header and cookie constraints become required parameters with a `const`
  schema. Path placeholders not bound by a `path_arg` are still declared;
  a greedy `{*name}` renders as `{name}` with a note.
* A container parameter fed by `query_arg` is a comma-separated list in
  one occurrence (`explode: false`); `query_list_arg` collects repeated
  keys, the `form`/`explode` default.
* `with_default` values render as `default`; `constrained(provider,
  {...})` renders its rules as JSON Schema keywords (`minimum`,
  `pattern`, `enum`, `minItems`, `format`, `examples`, ...) and enforces
  them at request time on every surface (`400`, `-32602`, tool error),
  so the document and the behaviour cannot drift apart.
* `add_security_scheme` fills `components.securitySchemes`;
  `set_security` and `operation::security` state requirements, and a
  route or provider reading a scheme's header, cookie or query parameter
  (or `Authorization` for an `http` scheme) is documented as that
  requirement instead of a parameter. `anonymous` emits `security: []`.
* `boost::describe`d types carry a `title` and are hoisted into
  `components.schemas`, where parameters and responses refer to them by
  JSON Reference; `set_inline_schemas` keeps them in place.
* The response schema is derived from the callable's return type when the
  built-in conversions apply; a hand-written `http::response` documents
  only a generic `200`. Per-operation `responses` override the default.
* Schemas come from `json_schema_for<T>()`: `boost::describe`d structs
  render their members (optional members are not required), described
  enums render as string enumerations, sequences and string-keyed maps
  render `items` and `additionalProperties`. MCP `inputSchema` uses the
  same function, so both documents agree by construction.

[endsect]

[section:serving_files Serving files]

`handler::htdocs` is a static file handler meant to face the internet
directly. Its rules follow the HTTP semantics of RFC 9110 rather than any
particular server's configuration language.

[heading Resolution and safety]

The file is the route's greedy capture (`target == "/static/{*path}"`
serves `root/app.css` for `/static/app.css`), selected as the `{*}`
entry or the only path argument, or named by `htdocs_options::path_arg`
when a route has several placeholders; without path arguments the whole
request path is used. The query never takes part.

* The path is percent-decoded by Boost.URL. An encoded `/` (`%2F`),
  backslash (`%5C`) or NUL (`%00`) anywhere in the path, and any `.` or
  `..` segment after decoding, answer 400. Names merely containing dots
  (`a..b.txt`) are fine.
* The resolved file, symbolic links followed, must lie under the
  canonical root; a link pointing outside answers 404 unless
  `follow_symlinks_outside_root`. Names starting with `.` answer 404
  unless `serve_hidden`; `.well-known` is always served. Refusals are
  404, not 403, so the tree cannot be probed.
* Only regular files are served: a directory requested without a
  trailing slash is redirected (301, query kept) to the slash form; with
  the slash the `index_files` are tried in order, and none means 404.
  There are no directory listings.
* GET and HEAD are served, anything else is 405 with `Allow: GET, HEAD`.
* Error responses are fixed `text/plain` reason phrases. They never echo
  the request, so a crafted path cannot inject markup.
* `fallback_document` turns misses into the application shell for
  single-page applications: a missing path whose last segment has no
  extension (or that `fallback_when` accepts) gets the document with
  `Cache-Control: no-cache`; missing assets stay 404.

Every response is a typed `http::response<segmented_body>`, so the
session's response filter and HEAD handling apply to files and errors
alike, and `with_session` and `guard` can wrap the handler. The
`on_response` hook sees the header of every served file (200, 206, 304)
for per-file additions such as `Content-Disposition`.

[heading Content types]

The media type comes from the last extension, case-insensitively: first
`mime_types` (user additions and overrides), then a built-in table of
web, image, font, audio, video, archive and office formats (`mime.hpp`),
else `default_mime_type`. Text types get `; charset=utf-8` unless the
configured value already has a parameter. `X-Content-Type-Options:
nosniff` accompanies every served file unless `nosniff` is turned off.

[heading Validators and conditional requests]

`Last-Modified` is the modification time at second resolution (never
later than the current time). The `ETag` is strong and derived from size
and modification time, plus the content coding for a precompressed
variant, so every representation has its own validator. Preconditions
are evaluated in the order RFC 9110 13.2.2 prescribes:

# `If-Match` (strong comparison, `*` and lists): 412 when nothing matches;
# otherwise `If-Unmodified-Since`: 412 when modified since;
# `If-None-Match` (weak comparison): 304 when a tag matches;
# otherwise `If-Modified-Since`: 304 when not modified since.

Invalid dates are ignored. A 304 carries `ETag`, `Last-Modified`,
`Cache-Control` and `Vary` and no body or content metadata.

`Cache-Control` comes from `cache_rules`, a first-match list of
predicates or globs on the relative path, then `default_cache_control`
(none by default). The common front-end policy is one call:
`cache_rules::immutable_hashed()` caches names carrying a content hash
(`app.3f9a1c2e.js`, `chunk-5ZQ3X2AB.js`) for a year as `immutable` and
sends `no-cache` for HTML. The pattern is: emit hashed names from the
build, reference them from HTML that is revalidated on every load, and a
deployment is picked up by the next page load while assets never need
revalidation.

[heading Ranges]

Full responses announce `Accept-Ranges: bytes`. A single range answers
206 with `Content-Range`; several answer `multipart/byteranges` with a
random boundary, after overlapping and adjacent ranges are merged. More
than `max_ranges` specs (16) or a malformed header are ignored and the
whole file is sent; a header with no satisfiable range answers 416 with
`Content-Range: bytes */size`. `If-Range` holds with a strong tag that
matches or a date equal to `Last-Modified`; otherwise the whole file is
sent. Ranges are byte windows of the open file: nothing is buffered.

[heading Precompressed variants]

With `precompressed = {"br", "gzip"}` (the default) a file `app.js` with
a sibling `app.js.br` or `app.js.gz` that is not older than the original
is served with `Content-Encoding` when `Accept-Encoding` accepts the
coding. The highest q wins, ties go to the configured order, and
identity wins only when the client ranks it higher explicitly;
`identity;q=0` is respected. A request without `Accept-Encoding` gets
identity. Files with a sibling carry `Vary: Accept-Encoding`, on 304
too. A request with `Range` is served from identity so that offsets
always refer to the uncompressed bytes. Nothing is compressed on the
fly.

[heading Caches]

A metadata cache (resolution, stat results, variants, validators, media
type) keyed by the decoded relative path holds `metadata_cache_entries`
(4096) entries and checks the file system again on the first use after
`revalidate_after` (1 s). Within that window a changed file can still be
described by its previous validators; a file whose size changed or that
vanished is noticed when it is opened and looked up afresh, so a body
never contradicts its `Content-Length`. Files up to
`memory_cache_max_file_size` (64 KiB) are kept in memory, up to
`memory_cache_max_total` (32 MiB) in all, and served from a shared
immutable buffer. Copies of a handler share the caches behind one mutex,
which is what lets one handler serve every connection strand.

[heading Blocking file I/O]

stat, open and read are synchronous calls on the connection's thread;
HEAD and 304 never open the file and the caches absorb most stats, but a
slow disk still stalls the thread that serves the request. Size the
thread pool accordingly. Asynchronous file I/O (Asio `stream_file`,
io_uring) and `sendfile` are future work.

[heading Out of scope]

Deliberately not provided: directory listings, on-the-fly compression,
bandwidth throttling, `.htaccess` files and rewrite rules (use matchers
and several routes), access control (use interceptors and guards),
custom error pages beyond the soft error handler and the response
filter, WebDAV, `sendfile` and asynchronous file I/O.

[endsect]

[endsect]
